            "complete": true,
            "remaining": [],
            "summary": "I fixed `csv.DictReader` and `csv.DictWriter` so a one-shot iterator passed as `fieldnames` now works for the header and for every row after it. The visible check and my 9 new tests all pass (10 tests in total).\n\n**What changed in `csv.py`:** in both constructors, if `fieldnames` isn't None and `iter(fieldnames) is fieldnames` (meaning it's a one-shot iterator), it's turned into a list once. Lists, tuples and other reusable sequences are stored as the exact object you passed in, so if you update a list later, the reader or writer sees the change. Non-iterable values like `1`, `object()` or `3.5` now raise TypeError when you construct the object, because `iter()` fails on them. `DictReader` with no `fieldnames` still reads the header row from the file. Nothing else in `csv.py` was touched.\n\n**Tests:** I added `tests/test_fieldnames_iter.py`. It loads the local `csv.py` directly and checks:\n- `DictReader` with an iterator or generator across several rows, with `restkey`/`restval`.\n- That a list passed to `DictReader` is kept as the same object and later changes to it take effect, plus tuples and the default header behaviour.\n- `DictWriter` with an iterator: `writeheader`, `writerow` and `writerows`, keeping your field order and quoting commas, embedded quotes, newlines and non-ASCII text (\u00e9\u20ac) correctly.\n- `DictWriter` still rejects extra keys by default (repeatedly, not just the first time) and ignores them when asked, with `restval` filling the gaps.\n- That a list passed to `DictWriter` is kept as the same object.\n- TypeError for non-iterable values on both classes.\n\n`tests/test_visible.py` is unchanged and still passes.\n\n**Decision for you:** `DictWriter(f, None)` still constructs without error, as it did before; I didn't make it raise TypeError, which would follow the \"non-iterable raises TypeError\" rule literally. If you want None rejected there, it's a one-line change.\n\nAlso note I didn't run the tests against the original code to watch the new iterator tests fail first. That they would fail is my reasoning from reading the code, not something I ran: the old writer used up the iterator on the first row. The full CPython test suite wasn't run (it was out of scope), and the independent owner checks haven't run yet."
          },
